The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions and statements of its President concerning the Central African Republic, in particular resolutions 2127 (2013), 2134 (2014), 2399 (2018), 2454 (2019), 2507 (2020), 2552 (2020), 2588 (2021), 2648 (2022), 2693 (2023) and 2745 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and unity of the Central African Republic, and recalling the importance of the principles of non-interference in the internal affairs of States, good-neighbourliness and regional cooperation,

Recalling the 2019 Political Agreement for Peace and Reconciliation in the Central African Republic and the need for its full and effective implementation, and stressing the importance of an inclusive political process and of transparent, credible and peaceful elections in accordance with national legal timelines,

Welcoming the progress made by the Central African Republic, including the continued efforts of the Government to extend State authority, to provide security and basic services, and to implement national reforms, and encouraging further progress, including in the areas of governance, justice, human rights and the fight against impunity,

Expressing grave concern at the continued presence and activity of armed groups and other actors in the Central African Republic, including foreign fighters and mercenaries, and their negative impact on peace, security, stability, human rights and the humanitarian situation, and condemning in the strongest terms all violations of international humanitarian law and all violations and abuses of human rights, including those involving sexual and gender-based violence, the recruitment and use of children, and attacks on civilians, humanitarian personnel and United Nations personnel,

Expressing concern at the illicit transfer, diversion and trafficking of arms and ammunition, including to armed groups, and at the negative impact of the illicit exploitation and trade of natural resources on peace and stability, and emphasizing the need for effective implementation of the targeted sanctions measures,

Recalling the humanitarian exemption to asset freeze measures established by resolution 2664 (2022), and emphasizing the need to ensure that humanitarian assistance reaches all those in need in accordance with the principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,

Reaffirming the importance of fair and clear procedures for the placement of individuals and entities on sanctions lists and for delisting, and welcoming the work of the Focal Point for Delisting,

Welcoming the work of the Panel of Experts and taking note of its reports and recommendations, and stressing the importance of the Panel’s continued cooperation with the Government of the Central African Republic and other relevant stakeholders,

Determining that the situation in the Central African Republic continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Recalls its resolution 2745 (2024), by which it lifted the arms embargo imposed by paragraph 1 of resolution 2127 (2013) and renewed the targeted sanctions measures, and reaffirms that the measures set out in paragraph 2 below continue to apply to individuals and entities designated by the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2127 (2013);

2. Decides to renew, until 31 July 2026, the travel ban and asset freeze measures imposed by paragraphs 9 and 12 of resolution 2134 (2014), as renewed and modified by subsequent resolutions, and reaffirms that these measures continue to apply to individuals and entities designated by the Committee;

3. Reaffirms that the asset freeze measures set out in paragraph 2 above shall not apply to the provision, processing or payment of funds, other financial assets or economic resources, or the provision of goods and services necessary to ensure the timely delivery of humanitarian assistance or to support other activities that support basic human needs, as provided in resolution 2664 (2022);

4. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 2127 (2013) until 31 August 2026, and requests the Secretary-General to take the necessary measures to ensure that the Panel has the necessary expertise and resources to fulfil its mandate;

5. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide a midterm report to the Committee by 15 April 2026 and a final report to the Council by 15 July 2026, and further requests the Panel to provide updates to the Committee as appropriate and to include in its reports information on the implementation of the measures, instances of non-compliance, and recommendations for improving the effectiveness of the measures;

6. Calls upon all Member States, relevant United Nations bodies, regional and subregional organizations, and other stakeholders to cooperate fully with the Committee and the Panel of Experts, including by providing information on the implementation of the measures and on potential violations;

7. Stresses the importance of the Government of the Central African Republic continuing to engage with the Panel of Experts and the Committee, and encourages the Government to continue its efforts to ensure the effective management and control of arms and ammunition, including through the strengthening of national institutions, legal frameworks and accountability mechanisms;

8. Reaffirms its intention to keep the measures under continuous review, and expresses its readiness to adjust them, including to delist individuals and entities where appropriate, in light of progress made on the ground;

9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

